From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MI50 ROCm compatibility project <mi50@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PyTorch: report native BF16 accurately on gfx906

PyTorch's ROCm implementation historically returns true from
torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported() without checking the device. Vega20/gfx906 has
no native BF16 arithmetic, so an MI50 build must not advertise the capability.
Keep the upstream behavior for other ROCm architectures, but inspect the
native device name in this downstream build and return false for gfx906.

The emulation probe remains available to callers that explicitly request it;
the default capability query now describes native hardware support.
---
 torch/cuda/__init__.py | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/torch/cuda/__init__.py b/torch/cuda/__init__.py
--- a/torch/cuda/__init__.py
+++ b/torch/cuda/__init__.py
@@ -198,8 +198,15 @@ def is_bf16_supported(including_emulation: bool = True):
     r"""Return a bool indicating if the current CUDA/ROCm device supports dtype bfloat16."""
-    # Check for ROCm, if true return true, no ROCM_VERSION check required,
-    # since it is supported on AMD GPU archs.
+    # ROCm devices differ: Vega20/gfx906 has no native BF16 arithmetic even
+    # though HIP can represent the dtype through conversion/emulation. Keep
+    # this capability query honest for the MI50 compatibility build.
     if torch.version.hip:
-        return True
+        if not is_available():
+            return False
+        device = torch.cuda.current_device()
+        arch = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(device).gcnArchName
+        if arch.startswith("gfx906"):
+            return False
+        return True
 
     # If CUDA is not available, than it does not support bf16 either
     if not is_available():
--
